      <h1>How to Play Cricket Darts</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">While 501 is the global tournament standard seen on television, <strong>Cricket</strong> is arguably the most popular casual and league dart game played across North America and in soft-tip darts worldwide. </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="Cricket Darts Rules" />

      <section class="content-card">

<p>Cricket is a tactical game of territory and timing. Rather than counting down from a large total to zero, players compete to claim ("close") seven specific target areas on the board while accumulating points on open targets before their opponent can shut them down. </p>
<p>Whether you are stepping up to a pub board on league night or practicing precision clustering at home, this complete guide covers the rules, the scoring notation, a full turn-by-turn worked example, and the strategic choices between attacking for points and defending your lead.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Cricket at a glance (numbers in play, goal of the game)</h2>
<p>In standard Cricket, only seven targets on the dartboard are active. All other segments on the board are out of play.</p>
<p><code>+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|               ACTIVE CRICKET TARGETS (7 TOTAL)              |
|                                                             |
|    [ 20 ]   [ 19 ]   [ 18 ]   [ 17 ]   [ 16 ]   [ 15 ]      |
|                                                             |
|                       [ BULLSEYE ]                          |
|             (Outer Bull = 25  |  Inner Bull = 50)           |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+</code></p>
<h3>The Active Numbers</h3>
<p>The only numbers in play are <strong>20, 19, 18, 17, 16, 15</strong>, and the <strong>Bullseye</strong>. </p>
<p>If a dart lands in any segment numbered 1 through 14, or in the outer ring outside the doubles wire, it scores nothing: no marks, no points, and no effect on the game state.</p>
<h3>The Objective of the Game</h3>
<p>To win a standard game of Cricket, a player (or team) must achieve two conditions:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Close all seven targets:</strong> You must register three "marks" on 20, 19, 18, 17, 16, 15, and the Bullseye.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Have equal or greater points:</strong> Your total point score must be equal to or higher than your opponent's score when your final target is closed.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>If you close every number first but trail on points, the game continues. You cannot claim victory until you score enough points on remaining open numbers to equal or exceed your opponent's tally.</p>
<h3>How Marks Are Counted</h3>
<p>To close any active target, a player must land <strong>three marks</strong> in that number. The board segments award marks based on their ring multiplier:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Single ring segment:</strong> 1 mark</li>
<li><strong>Double ring segment (outer thin ring):</strong> 2 marks</li>
<li><strong>Treble ring segment (inner thin ring):</strong> 3 marks (closes the number in a single dart)</li>
<li><strong>Outer Bull (single green bull, 25 points):</strong> 1 mark</li>
<li><strong>Inner Bull (double red bull, 50 points):</strong> 2 marks</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <AdSlot position="in-article" />
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Setup &amp; turn order</h2>
<p>Setting up a game of Cricket follows the same standard board dimensions and throwing regulations governed by the <a href="/darts/rules/">DRA standard rules of darts</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Board height:</strong> The centre of the bullseye hangs <strong>5 ft 8 in (1.73 m)</strong> from the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Throwing distance:</strong> The front toe line (oche) sits <strong>7 ft 9¼ in (2.37 m)</strong> measured horizontally along the floor from the face of the board.</li>
<li><strong>Darts per turn:</strong> Each player delivers three darts per turn, retrieving them only after all three darts have been thrown and scored.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Determining Who Throws First</h3>
<p>Players decide starting order by throwing a single dart at the bullseye (known as "diddling for the middle" or "corking"). The player whose dart lands closest to the centre of the inner bull throws first. If both players hit the outer bull, both hit the inner bull, or the distance is too close to judge by eye, both retrieve their darts and re-throw in reverse order.</p>
<h3>The Scoreboard Layout</h3>
<p>A Cricket scoreboard is typically drawn on a chalkboard, dry-erase board, or electronic scoring app with three columns:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Left column:</strong> Player 1's marks and points</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Middle column:</strong> The target numbers listed vertically in descending order: <strong>20, 19, 18, 17, 16, 15, B</strong></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Right column:</strong> Player 2's marks and points</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><code>Player 1       Target       Player 2
 -----------   ----------   -----------
                   20       
                   19       
                   18       
                   17       
                   16       
                   15       
                    B       
 -----------   ----------   -----------
   Pts: 0                      Pts: 0</code></p>
<h3>Traditional Scoring Symbols</h3>
<p>As players land darts on active targets, the scorekeeper records marks beside the corresponding number using a progressive 3-stroke system:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>First mark (1 hit):</strong> A single forward slash <code>/</code></li>
<li><strong>Second mark (2 hits):</strong> An <code>X</code> (formed by crossing the initial slash)</li>
<li><strong>Third mark (3 hits — Closed):</strong> A circle around the <code>X</code> (<code>⨂</code>)</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Closing a number vs pointing (scoring on your opponent)</h2>
<p>Understanding the distinction between <strong>closing</strong> a number and <strong>pointing</strong> is the core strategic foundation of Cricket.</p>
<p><code>PLAYER A HITS TARGET
                 |
                 v
     Has Player A closed it? (&lt; 3 marks)
       /                   \
     YES                    NO
      |                      |
Number is now OPEN     Add marks (/, X, or ⨂)
for Player A to point.  until 3 marks reached.
      |
      v
Has Player B ALSO closed it?
  /             \
YES              NO
 |               |
DEAD / DEADLOCK  POINT SCORING!
No points can    Player A scores segment value
be scored by     (e.g., S20 = 20 pts, T20 = 60 pts)
either player.   on every additional hit.</code></p>
<h3>1. Opening / Closing for Yourself</h3>
<p>When you land three marks on a number, that number is <strong>closed for you</strong>. This means two things:
* You have satisfied that number's requirement toward your ultimate winning condition.
* The number is now "open" for you to score points on, as long as your opponent has not yet closed it.</p>
<h3>2. Pointing (Scoring on an Opponent)</h3>
<p>Once you have closed a number, every subsequent hit you land on that target scores its point value, <strong>provided your opponent has fewer than three marks on it</strong>:
* Hitting a Single 20 after closing it awards <strong>20 points</strong>.
* Hitting a Treble 20 after closing it awards <strong>60 points</strong>.
* Hitting an Outer Bull after closing bullseyes awards <strong>25 points</strong>; an Inner Bull awards <strong>50 points</strong>.</p>
<p>These points are added to your running score total.</p>
<h3>3. Killing / Deadening a Number</h3>
<p>If your opponent also scores three marks on that same number, the number is now <strong>dead</strong> (fully closed by both sides). </p>
<p>Once a number is dead:
* Neither player can ever score points on it again for the remainder of the leg.
* Further hits on that segment are wasted darts.</p>
<h3>The Surplus Marks Rule</h3>
<p>If you hit a multiple segment that exceeds the three marks required to close a number, the excess marks immediately convert into points—provided your opponent has not closed it:
* <strong>Example:</strong> You have two marks on 20 (<code>X</code>). You throw a Treble 20 (3 marks). One mark is used to complete the close (<code>⨂</code>), and the remaining two marks immediately score <strong>40 points</strong> (2 × 20).
* If your opponent had already closed 20 before your throw, the Treble simply closes your 20, and the excess marks produce zero points because the number is dead.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Worked example: 6-turn sample game (scoring table)</h2>
<p>To see how closing, pointing, surplus marks, and defensive responses work in live play, follow this turn-by-turn breakdown between <strong>Player 1</strong> and <strong>Player 2</strong>.</p>
<h3>Turn-by-Turn Game Log</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Turn 1 (Player 1):</strong> Throws at 20.</li>
<li>Dart 1: Treble 20 (3 marks). Closes 20 immediately.</li>
<li>Dart 2: Single 20. Scores 20 points (P1 has 20 closed, P2 does not).</li>
<li>Dart 3: Single 20. Scores 20 points.</li>
<li>
<p><em>Result:</em> P1 closes 20 and leads 40–0.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn 2 (Player 2):</strong> Responds to P1's scoring threat on 20.</p>
</li>
<li>Dart 1: Single 20 (1 mark).</li>
<li>Dart 2: Treble 20 (3 marks). Two marks close 20 for P2; the leftover mark yields zero points because P1 already closed 20. Number 20 is now <strong>dead</strong>.</li>
<li>Dart 3: Switches to 19, hits Treble 19 (3 marks). Closes 19 immediately.</li>
<li>
<p><em>Result:</em> 20 is dead. P2 closes 19. Score remains P1 40, P2 0.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn 3 (Player 1):</strong> With 20 dead and P2 threatening to point on 19, P1 plays defense on 19.</p>
</li>
<li>Dart 1: Single 19 (1 mark).</li>
<li>Dart 2: Single 19 (1 mark, now 2 marks total).</li>
<li>Dart 3: Miss (lands in 3).</li>
<li>
<p><em>Result:</em> P1 has 2 marks on 19. 19 remains open for P2 to point.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn 4 (Player 2):</strong> Takes advantage of open 19 to attack for points.</p>
</li>
<li>Dart 1: Treble 19. Scores 57 points.</li>
<li>Dart 2: Single 19. Scores 19 points.</li>
<li>Dart 3: Switches to 18, hits Single 18 (1 mark).</li>
<li>
<p><em>Result:</em> P2 accumulates 76 points on 19 and now leads 76–40.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn 5 (Player 1):</strong> Must kill 19 to stop P2 from pointing, then opens 18.</p>
</li>
<li>Dart 1: Single 19 (1 mark). Closes 19 for P1. 19 is now <strong>dead</strong>.</li>
<li>Dart 2: Treble 18 (3 marks). Closes 18 for P1.</li>
<li>Dart 3: Single 18. Scores 18 points (P2 only has 1 mark on 18).</li>
<li>
<p><em>Result:</em> 19 is dead. P1 closes 18 and scores 18 points. Score is now P1 58, P2 76.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn 6 (Player 2):</strong> Defends 18 and attacks 17.</p>
</li>
<li>Dart 1: Double 18 (2 marks). Closes 18 for P2. 18 is now <strong>dead</strong>.</li>
<li>Dart 2: Treble 17 (3 marks). Closes 17 for P2.</li>
<li>Dart 3: Single 17. Scores 17 points.</li>
<li><em>Result:</em> 18 is dead. P2 closes 17 and adds 17 points. Score is P1 58, P2 93.</li>
</ul>
