      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Winning &amp; common house rules / variants</h2>
<p>A standard leg of Cricket ends the instant a player completes both winning requirements: all seven targets closed, and points greater than or equal to their opponent.</p>
<p><code>+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                      WIN CHECK DECISION                     |
|                                                             |
|   1. Have you closed 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, and Bull?      |
|      -&gt; YES: Proceed to Step 2.                             |
|      -&gt; NO:  Game continues.                                |
|                                                             |
|   2. Are your points &gt;= opponent's points?                  |
|      -&gt; YES: YOU WIN THE GAME!                              |
|      -&gt; NO:  Game continues. You must score points on       |
|              remaining unclosed numbers until ahead.        |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+</code></p>
<h3>Common Variants and House Rules</h3>
<p>Depending on whether you are playing in a league, a pub, or on an electronic soft-tip machine, you will encounter several established variants:</p>
<h4>1. Cut-Throat Cricket (A Common Variant for 3+ Players)</h4>
<p>In standard two-player Cricket, pointing adds points to your own tally. In <strong>Cut-Throat Cricket</strong>—a common variant when three or more players compete—the scoring rule flips:
* When you score on a number you have closed, points are awarded to <strong>every opponent who has not closed that number</strong>.
* The winning condition reverses: you must close all numbers and have the <strong>lowest</strong> (or zero) point total.
* Cut-Throat prevents collusion in multi-player games, because pointing directly penalizes opponents who are lagging behind on marks.</p>
<h4>2. No-Score Cricket (Simple Cricket)</h4>
<p>Points are completely eliminated. Players simply race to be the first to close all seven targets (15 through 20 and Bullseye). 
* The first person to place three marks on all seven targets wins immediately.
* This format is ideal for beginners focusing purely on board navigation and cluster accuracy without the arithmetic of points.</p>
<h4>3. Scram (Stopper vs. Scorer)</h4>
<p>A two-player practice variant played across two halves:
* <strong>First half:</strong> Player 1 is the "Stopper" and tries to close numbers 1–20 and the Bull. Player 2 is the "Scorer" and scores points on any number Player 1 has not yet closed. Once the Stopper closes everything, the round ends and points are tallied.
* <strong>Second half:</strong> Roles reverse. Player 2 becomes the Stopper and Player 1 becomes the Scorer.
* The player with the highest points at the end of their scoring half wins.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Cricket vs 501 - which to learn first</h2>
<p>Beginners often wonder whether they should concentrate on Cricket or standard 501. While both games use the same dartboard and throwing fundamentals, they train distinctly different aspects of a dart player's skillset.</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Feature</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Cricket Darts</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">501 Darts</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Primary Goal</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Close 7 targets &amp; hold points advantage</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Count down from 501 to exactly 0</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Finishing Requirement</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Any mark on last target (if ahead on points)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Must finish on a Double segment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Numbers Utilized</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">15–20 and Bullseye</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Full board (focus on T20, T19, and outer doubles)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Bust Rule Applied?</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">No <a href="/darts/bust-rule-in-darts/">bust rule</a> (misses simply score 0 marks)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Yes (scoring more than remaining total busts turn)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Math Complexity</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Addition of segment values (e.g. 20, 40, 60)</td>
