</table></div>
<h3>Which Game Should You Start With?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start with Cricket if:</strong> You are playing in North America, enjoy social head-to-head pub matches, or want to develop repeatable grouping on the board's largest scoring segments (treble 20 down to treble 15). Cricket gives beginners immediate target feedback without the frustration of repeated checkout misses.</li>
<li><strong>Start with 501 if:</strong> You aspire to play in official tournament streams, want to follow PDC/WDF competitive circuits, or want to master the double-out finish from day one.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Coaching Recommendation:</strong> Intermediate players should practice both. Cricket builds exceptional vertical accuracy across the top and bottom of the board, while 501 sharpens pressure finishes and arithmetic. You can incorporate both disciplines into your training using our structured <a href="/darts/practice-routines/">darts practice routines</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does a treble close a number in a single dart?</h3>
            <p>Yes. In Cricket, a treble segment counts as three marks. If you hit a Treble 20 on an unworked number, all three required marks are satisfied immediately, closing the number in one throw.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Can you win Cricket if you close all numbers but have fewer points?</h3>
            <p>No. You cannot win standard Cricket while trailing on points. If you close your final target (e.g., Bullseye) but your opponent leads 85 to 60, the game continues. You must use remaining open numbers where your opponent has fewer than three marks to score points until your total equals or exceeds your opponent's score.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What happens if you hit a number outside 15–20 or Bull?</h3>
            <p>Any dart landing in segments 1 through 14, or outside the double wire, is completely out of play. It registers no marks, awards no points, and simply counts as one of your three thrown darts for that turn.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is the difference between Outer Bull and Inner Bull in Cricket?</h3>
            <p>The green outer ring (Single Bull) counts as <strong>one mark</strong> and is worth 25 points if pointing. The red inner ring (Double Bull) counts as <strong>two marks</strong> and is worth 50 points if pointing. Hitting an inner bull and an outer bull in the same turn provides three marks, closing the bullseye in two darts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is the optimal basic strategy in Cricket?</h3>
            <p>The fundamental rule of thumb is: <strong>Points protect you, but closing wins the game.</strong> If you have an opportunity to point on a number your opponent has left open, establishing a 40- to 60-point buffer forces your opponent into a defensive posture, preventing them from running away with the leg.</p>
          </div>
      </section>

